### Decorator Pattern: Composition Over Inheritance

Decorator wraps an object behind the same interface it implements. It adds behavior around calls to the wrapped instance, without touching its source.

Inheritance bakes every combination of behavior into a fixed class hierarchy at compile time. Supporting logging, caching and validation as independent toggles would need 2³ = 8 subclasses. Decorator makes each concern a separate wrapper that composes at runtime: `new CachingDecorator(new LoggingDecorator(client))`.

```typescript
interface ApiClient {
  request(url: string): Promise<unknown>;
}

class BaseClient implements ApiClient {
  async request(url: string) { return fetch(url).then(r => r.json()); }
}

abstract class ApiClientDecorator implements ApiClient {
  constructor(protected wrapped: ApiClient) {}
  abstract request(url: string): Promise<unknown>;
}

class LoggingDecorator extends ApiClientDecorator {
  async request(url: string) {
    console.log(`-> ${url}`);
    const result = await this.wrapped.request(url);
    console.log(`<- ${url}`);
    return result;
  }
}

class CachingDecorator extends ApiClientDecorator {
  private cache = new Map<string, unknown>();
  async request(url: string) {
    if (this.cache.has(url)) return this.cache.get(url);
    const result = await this.wrapped.request(url);
    this.cache.set(url, result);
    return result;
  }
}

const client = new CachingDecorator(new LoggingDecorator(new BaseClient()));
```

The abstract `ApiClientDecorator` base class matters. It forces every decorator to implement the exact same `ApiClient` interface as the object it wraps. So any decorator can wrap any other decorator, or the base client, in any order. The whole stack is still assignable wherever an `ApiClient` is expected.

Now say a decorator implements only *some* of the wrapped interface's methods. For example, it forgets to forward a `cancel()` method that `BaseClient` has. Calling code that only knows about `ApiClient` will not notice at compile time.

But two kinds of code break the moment that decorator sits between them and the base object. One upcasts back to a concrete type. The other relies on a method the interface does not declare.

**Common pitfall:** writing a decorator that forwards only the methods the author remembered to think about. Every decorator must forward the *entire* interface, unless it deliberately intercepts a method. An incomplete decorator works fine in isolation. It drops functionality silently as soon as it joins a stack with other decorators, or is consumed through the shared interface type.

