### Mediator Pattern: Centralizing Component Communication

Mediator introduces a central object that components talk to, instead of talking to each other directly. Each component knows only the mediator, never its siblings. Adding a new component means teaching the mediator about it, not wiring direct references between every pair that might need to interact.

```typescript
interface FormField {
  getValue(): unknown;
  setError(message: string | null): void;
}

class FormMediator {
  private fields = new Map<string, FormField>();

  register(name: string, field: FormField) { this.fields.set(name, field); }

  onFieldChange(name: string) {
    if (name === "startDate" || name === "endDate") {
      const start = this.fields.get("startDate")?.getValue() as Date;
      const end = this.fields.get("endDate")?.getValue() as Date;
      if (start && end && start > end) {
        this.fields.get("endDate")?.setError("End date must be after start date");
      } else {
        this.fields.get("endDate")?.setError(null);
      }
    }
  }
}

class DateField implements FormField {
  constructor(
    private name: string,
    private mediator: FormMediator,
    private value: Date | null = null
  ) {
    mediator.register(name, this);
  }
  getValue() { return this.value; }
  setError(message: string | null) { /* update UI */ }
  onChange(value: Date) { this.value = value; this.mediator.onFieldChange(this.name); }
}
```

Without a mediator, cross-validating fields directly means every field holds a reference to each field it must react to. Think of a form with N interdependent fields: start and end dates, password and confirmation, country and state, quantity and total.

If the validation rules are dense, that can require up to N×(N-1) direct references. Every new field then potentially needs wiring into every existing field that depends on it. That is the N² problem: the number of connections grows quadratically with the number of participants, not linearly.

Mediator has its own risk, and it mirrors the risk of a global event bus. Centralizing all interaction logic in one object removes the N² direct references. But the mediator then collects every cross-field rule in the system. It can grow into a god object of its own: one class that knows and does far too much.

Concretely, a single `onFieldChange` method keeps gaining `if` branches: one for every new validation rule, across every field pair in a large form. The N² problem does not disappear. It gets concentrated into one class's cyclomatic complexity, instead of being distributed and hidden across many pairwise references.

For a genuinely large form, an unstructured Mediator can be as hard to maintain as the direct-reference version was. The difference is that the mess sits in one file instead of being scattered across many.

**Common pitfall:** treating Mediator as a complete fix for tangled component communication, without also organizing the mediator's own logic. Split the validation rules into separate rule objects that the mediator dispatches to, rather than one growing conditional block. Mediator solves the *reference* explosion. It does nothing about the *logic* explosion if every rule still lives as another branch in one method.

